Z||000004||rockcrawl||11-26-2000||09:27 AM||||ACE and Design One do 4.6 Northstar swaps, the 4.9 is not a Northstar. It's an aluminum V8 with pushrods. I thought the intake sealing problems had been corrected by the time the 4.1 had evolved to the 4.5 and 4.9. Either way, I don't think it's a bad engine. <P> I have a 4.9 in my Rossa spyder project, but I used the Caddi cradle and a custom tube frame, so I can't help with mounts. I'd assume they'd all need to be custom made unless Chris West is selling them. <P>There are lots of shops that can make a custom flywheel for it, but prices are $500 and up (ouch!). I'll probably just shell out the big bucks and get an aluminum flywheel for mine. I think the flywheel is the same as a 3800 except it is nuetrally ballanced. So a flywheel for a 3800 Fiero might fit if re-ballanced. Mine's auto now, but I'll be converting to a Berreta Getrag with intermediate shaft. If you go auto, it will bolt right up with the original Caddi flexplate. With either trans I think you will have to notch the bell housing for starter clearance.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||11-26-2000||10:09 AM||||3800 Flywheels have to be custom made as the 3800 was never available with a standard shift.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||11-26-2000||12:11 PM||||Yes, a 3800 Fiero flywheel must be custom made, but it is a lot more common than a 4.9 flywheel. Also, there was a Series II 3800 used in the Camaro (1996?) that was available with a manual trans, but it is thicker and must be machined down to work in a Fiero. That may be cheaper than having one made from a chunk of steel as Team Python did. I'm still mot totally sure the 3800 has the same bolt pattern as the 4.9, and I don't have my 4.9 appart to measure it. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||11-26-2000||03:16 PM||||My 3800 ('97 Buick) bolts right up to the Getrag 5 sp. <BR>Rockcrawl<BR>Are you sure about the stick shift 3800 Camero?||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||11-26-2000||05:08 PM||||The 3800 uses an 8 bolt crank with one of the bolts being 3 degrees off to act as a key. I just remembered that the 4.9 uses six bolts, so the 3800 flywheel won't work on a 4.9. Not sure why I thought it was the same. <P>The F-body flywheel (p.n.#24503285) is 1.110 inch thick. This needs to be turned down to .840 inch to match the V6 Getrag flywheel thickness. Once done, this will work for a normally aspirated Series II 3800. Cost: new GM flywheel approximately $160 from <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gmpartsdirect.com/</A>  plus machining. <P>Jon||||reg||1
Z||000009||Pontiaddict||11-26-2000||05:50 PM||||Okay, for the trans. You have to clearance the bellhousing for starter clearance. On a 4 speed, I think you have to modify the shifter bracket for clearance. Chris west has flywheels for about $250. The motor mounts are universal poly mounts. You have to cut the old motor mount tab on the engine cradle for exhaust clearance. If you don't relocate the engine accessories,(move them like they are on a 2.8) you have to notch the decklid to clear the alternator. <BR>You have to cut the dogbone mount and some of the decklid hinge bracket, but that has to be done on most engine swaps. then there is the electrical work, intake and exhaust routing and other stuff you haven't planned for. (It happens)<BR>It can be done inexpensively if you want to do alot of work yourself or scam someone into doing it for free.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com/Engine_Swaps/4_9cadswap.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cwestco.com/Engine_Swaps/4_9cadswap.html</A> ||||reg||1
